  "script": {
    "segments": [
      {
        "narration": "Around 300 dogs have jumped from the same Scottish bridge. Overtoun, near Dumbarton. Completed in 1895. A fifty-foot drop to the rocks below.",
        "visual_query": "scottish stone arch bridge",
        "visual_fallback": "old stone bridge"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Reports go back to the 1950s. Almost always the same side. Almost always the same stretch of wall. Around fifty of the dogs have died.",
        "visual_query": "west scotland woodland burn",
        "visual_fallback": "scottish woodland"
      },
      {
        "narration": "The Scottish SPCA investigated and came away with nothing conclusive. Which leaves the real question: what can a dog sense on a bridge that we cannot?",
        "visual_query": "stone parapet bridge moss",
        "visual_fallback": "stone parapet"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Smell. Canine psychologist David Sands found it at the base \u2014 mink. The RSPB found mink, mouse and squirrel nests on exactly the favoured side.",
        "visual_query": "scottish woodland undergrowth stream",
        "visual_fallback": "forest stream"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Male mink scent overwhelms a dog. And the bridge wall tapers, hiding the drop. So the dog leaps at prey it can smell but never sees.",
        "visual_query": "mossy stone wall forest",
        "visual_fallback": "mossy wall"
      }
    ],
